{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Own low-risk output transport experiments from the fastest B candidate (2f65bf3). Isolate buffer reservation/block-size and a single emission API (bulk ostream versus stdio, never mixed) plus iostream sync/tie settings. Preserve direct in-buffer record construction, exact bytes, bounded-memory/overflow fallbacks, and the empty-input behavior. Return only a valid, repeatedly measured improvement over that parent.\",\"B\":\"Own permutation/serialization hot-path research. Compare the measured direct-construction writer with an incremental reusable-record design that updates only slots changed by a lexicographic successor; a custom successor may expose the changed suffix. Keep sorting, duplicate handling, byte comparison behavior, and every serialized record exact; do not use counts, expected inputs, or unbounded precomputation. Retain the direct writer if incremental maintenance loses.\",\"C\":\"Own the distinct batching/parallelization hypothesis. Prior bounded OpenMP record batches were slower (about 0.00692-0.00700 s versus 0.00655 s), so require evidence before retaining threads. Explore only bounded ordered batches with independent record ownership, single ordered output, safe allocation/overflow fallbacks, and a conservative serial path. Investigate thresholds and formatting layout rather than combining other trees' I/O edits; report a valid candidate only if it beats the best serial parent.\",\"G\":\"Integrate empirically, beginning with B commit 2f65bf3deab86d6069c5a00324113f173236dd96 (0.006550 s). Treat A's writer variants, B's incremental scheme, and C's batching as competing alternatives unless an orthogonal composition is rebuilt and measured; do not assume gains add. Validate exact ordered output for whitespace removal, empty input, repeated characters, and ordinary nonempty inputs, retain bounded-memory guards, then select only the fastest valid repeated measurement under the unchanged environment.\",\"analysis\":\"Baseline is 0.142898 s; all admissible variants show that eliminating per-character formatted insertion and endl flushing dominates. The current leader is B's direct pending-buffer serializer at 0.006550 s, narrowly ahead of A's reusable fwrite line at 0.006654 s. The OpenMP/batch approaches and simpler buffered writer are slower, so the main opportunity is careful serial output and per-permutation work reduction, with parallel formatting treated as a skeptical experiment. All work must preserve the source's precise whitespace stripping, lexicographic distinct-permutation sequence, and output bytes for every valid input.\"}"}}
